{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1chpcEBeo7v7h4GUp64","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I did not change `/work/putnam_2024_a4.lean`, so it still contains the original `sorry`.\n\nThe statement is the full characterization of the set of primes, which is Putnam 2024 A4 with answer {7}. Both directions need real work, and I did not get a compiling proof.\n\n- **p = 7 (membership):** this is easy. Take a = 3, r = 1, and e = (0, 2, 1). The sequence is 1, 9 ≡ 2, 3 (mod 7), which has constant difference 1. The remaining work is the bijection and congruence checks in Lean.\n- **Exclusion of p > 7:** this is the hard direction. The argument I worked out on paper is:\n  1. If a ≡ 0 (mod p), the multiset of powers is {1, 0, …, 0} with at least two zeros, but an arithmetic progression of p − 4 < p terms with nonzero difference has no repeats. Contradiction.\n  2. Otherwise, the progression b_0 + n·r (n = 0..p−5) has distinct residues, so the powers a^0, …, a^{p−5} are distinct. This forces ord(a) ≥ p − 4, so ord(a) = p − 1 when p > 7.\n  3. The complement of the powers in ℤ/p is {0, u, u², u³} with u = a⁻¹, and this must be an arithmetic progression of length 4 after scaling. That puts three nonzero residues in {±1, ±2, ±3} with β² ≡ αγ (mod p). Checking the four consecutive-with-0 patterns gives no integer solutions, so for p ≥ 19 this is a contradiction. The primes 11, 13, and 17 need a separate finite check.\n\nFormalizing step 3 in Lean means handling Finset sums and bijections over `ZMod p`, orders of units, and the small-prime cases. I estimate that as a long, error-prone development that I did not complete."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":582,"cache_read_input_tokens":1881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":582,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tie